WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.200
Some people argue that large tech
companies are too big. They are indeed

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:14.070
large: Facebook has more active users
than there are people in China; Google handles

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:18.900
at least two trillion searches a year.
Why are these companies so big? Because

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.490
you and I use them. We do that because we
get lots of value. So saying they're too

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:28.019
big is to say they provide too much
value, which doesn't make sense. Other

00:00:28.019 --> 00:00:30.840
people argue the big tech makes too much money:

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:36.750
Facebook's 2017 profit was 16 billion
dollars, about 40 percent of its revenue.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:41.160
Does Facebook really need that much
money? That's the wrong question. Only

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:46.410
about 10 to 20 percent of tech firms
ever make a profit, so 80 to 90 percent

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:51.000
either break even or lose substantial
amounts of money. The profits of the few

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.800
drive investors to endure the losses of
the many. On average, the tech sector's

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.840
returns are comparable to those of the
S&amp;P 500. So when someone says that tech

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.150
companies are too big, say you're
grateful: You're getting great value and

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.080
competition for the future!

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.000
What do you
think? Should big tech be broken up?

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.119
Let us know your comments. Also, let us know
what other topics you'd like our

00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:20.580
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be
sure to like and subscribe for more

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.619
research and videos from AEI.

